Durarara!! AU
Chapter 22 – Part 3
Earlier
After Russia Sushi, Shinra and Mei did not come straight home.
Celty did.
The apartment was too quiet without their noise and too occupied by the night they had already left in it. She set her helmet down, then did the one careful thing first: both fortune slips went away together. A lost object will be found. What you leave behind will follow you anyway. Not in the trash. Not on the table where Mei could pick them up later. Folded, kept, hers for now.
Then she tidied.
It should have been ordinary. Dishes. A cushion. The living room returned to a shape that did not still smell, to her, like anything at all. She could not catch the air the way Curtis had at the sushi counter. She had only rooms and objects.
That was enough.
The couch had an odd stain. Not food. Not tea. A mark that did not belong to her sitting there in riding gear, or to Shinra eating over notes. In the office it was worse and more specific: another stain on Shinra’s chair, then blotches on the floor near the desk, as if someone had cleaned in a hurry and not well enough for a person who actually looked.
Celty stood over the desk for a long second, black smoke rising in a thin, ugly ribbon from the empty space above her collar.
She took pictures.
Not to be theatrical. To keep the evidence from becoming a feeling she could later be talked out of. Couch. Chair. Floor. The angle of the desk. She put the phone away and did not throw the cushions or tear the room apart. She finished tidying around the proof instead, which was somehow colder.
She planned to text Curtis later.
He had already looked at Mei like evidence. He had refused the address. He had called the table an interrogation. If anyone in the city would not laugh this into Mei being Mei, it was the boy who had walked out of Russia Sushi after paying for a dinner he barely ate.

Present
After Anri left, the suite’s peace lasted the length of a closed door.
Curtis was still in the red cap and black shirt, the doorway hug still in his arms, when the phone lit.
Celty.
He almost smiled at the name. Then he opened it.
Celty:
Are you alone.
Celty:
I found something at home.
Couch. Shinra’s office chair. Floor by the desk.
The photos followed, clinical and unmistakable even without a caption.
Celty:
They stayed out after sushi.
I can’t smell it. I can see it.
A last line, tighter than the kind message from earlier.
Celty:
You already knew something was off with her.
Tell me if I’m looking at what I think I’m looking at.
Curtis stood in the quiet flat Anri had just left, later hour still warm in the room, and stared at a set of images that dragged Russia Sushi, Mei’s “full body physical,” and Shinra’s delighted laugh straight back through his front door.
The peace was over.
Curtis stared at the photos until the doorway hug finished dying in his hands.
Then he sighed, red cap tipping forward, and said it to the empty suite like the furniture might take the news better than he would.
“…Fuck all kinds of duck.”
A shorter, thinner breath. He thumbed back to the office-chair shot, then the blotches by the desk, the same desk Shinra had been delighted at all evening.
“Shinra, what did you do…” The name left him low, almost pleading with a man who was not in the room. “Please tell me you and Mei are just friends…”
He already knew they were not just that. He had smelled it at Russia Sushi and filed it wrong on purpose, hoping the file would stay at old classmate, leftover heat, something that could still be walked back into manners. The pictures did not walk back. They put the current on the furniture Celty had to live with, in a house she could not even smell the betrayal in.
Curtis sat down hard on his own couch — the clean one Anri had just left — and looked at Celty’s last line again.
You already knew something was off with her.
Tell me if I’m looking at what I think I’m looking at.
He dragged a hand down his face.
Friends would have been easier.
Friends would have let him answer without becoming the person who confirmed the shape of her home to her.
He typed nothing yet. The plea to Shinra stayed in the air of the suite, useless and already too late, while the boy who had just said he liked Anri a lot now had to decide whether to tell a headless woman the thing her eyes had already found.
Another message came through before he could invent a kinder answer to the photos.
Celty:
I’m coming over.
Whether you’re sleeping or not.
Curtis looked at the line, then at the still-warm impression of Anri on his couch, then at himself: red cap, black shirt, red tie, white suspenders, a later hour that had just been a doorway hug and was about to become a second visit from a woman carrying pictures of her own home.
“…Of course you are,” he muttered.
He did not tell her no. After the tower, the open door, the cookie, and are you alone, a refusal would have sounded like hiding. He also did not have time to change the whole night off his clothes. He typed back the only useful thing.
Curtis:
im awake.
door’ll be open.
Then he stood there in the furnished quiet, photos still on the screen, Anri’s tea cup not yet washed, and the knowledge that Celty was already moving toward the one address Mei had not been allowed to have.
The peace after the hug was officially finished.
The next knock would not be asking if he liked anyone.
It would be asking him to name the stain.
A while later Celty arrived.
The door was open as promised. She stepped in without knocking, yellow helmet under one arm, the night air still on her riding suit, and stopped just inside at the sight of him: red cap, black shirt, red tie, white suspenders, a book in his hands and the television on low like the evening had been allowed to become ordinary. It had not. She could see the leftover second cup on the table. She could see he had dressed for someone.
Curtis closed the book.
“Welcome, Celty.”
For a second she only stood there, black smoke rising in a thin restless ribbon from the empty space above her collar. The private channel opened before the PDA did, because this was not a courier greeting.
You look like you already had company.
Then, out loud in the only voice he would hear that way, lower and stripped of dinner manners:
“I didn’t come to be welcomed.” A short pause as she set the helmet down harder than necessary. “I came because my house has marks I did not make, and you sat across from that woman tonight like you already knew the air was wrong.”
She pulled the phone, not to hide behind it, just to put the photos back in the room with them.
“Tell me what I’m looking at, Curtis. Don’t tidy it. Don’t call them friends to be kind to me. I kept your fortune. I shut her down when she asked for this address. Now I need you to say the thing I can see and cannot smell.”
The empty line of her neck stayed on him, on the cleaner evening look, on the book he had closed like a host.
“And if someone just left… I don’t care. I’m still here. Answer me.”
Curtis blinked a few times, red cap tipping as if the welcome he had offered had been slapped out of his hands.
“…That’s rude, Celty.”
Not loud. Just startled, the host version of him colliding with the woman who had walked into his open door and gone straight through the later hour like it was furniture in the way of a crime scene.
Celty did not take it back.
Black smoke jerked once above her collar. The private channel came through tight and unapologetic.
Rude is finding another woman’s night on the furniture I live with and then watching you blink at me for not saying hello nicely.
A short, sharper beat.
“You can call me rude. Fine. I came from a house I had to photograph. You were the only person I trusted not to laugh it into nothing.” Her gloved hand flexed at her side. “I am not here to insult your company or your book. I am here because if I go back to Shinra and Mei with only a feeling, they will talk me out of my own eyes.”
The empty line of her neck stayed on him.
“So yes. I skipped welcome. I skipped the part where I ask how your evening was. That is rude.” A thinner pause, less apology than fact. “I will be polite after you tell me what those pictures are. Not before.”
Curtis was quiet.
He did not pile onto that’s rude. He did not defend the second cup or the cleaner clothes. He just sat there with the closed book on his knee, red cap shadowing the mismatched eyes, and let the suite hold the fact that she had walked in already mid-fight with a house he did not live in.
The silence did the work. Celty felt it land.
Black smoke thinned, then rose again, less attack than regret. The private channel came back lower.
…I’m sorry.
She said it twice — once in the link, once with the PDA lifted a fraction as if the habit of texting the apology might make it safer.
Celty:
I’m sorry.
That was rude.
A short, rougher pause in the air between them.
“I shouldn’t have come through your door like the room was already guilty. You opened it. You said welcome. I had no right to treat that as an obstacle.” The empty line of her neck dipped, not quite a bow. “I’m still scared of what those pictures are. That does not make it fair to take it out on you for having a quiet night.”
She stayed where she was, helmet on the table, the photos unsent again but still in her hand.
“I’m sorry, Curtis. I want the answer. I should have asked for it without stepping on you first.”
Curtis sighed.
“…Alright.”
He flipped his phone open and went through the pictures again, red cap tipping as the host version of him stepped aside for the one that treated a stain like evidence instead of a feeling. Couch. Chair. Floor by the desk. He studied them the way he had studied Mei at the sushi counter, only this time the file had arrived in his own living room.
“Were you able to scrape anything off?” The question was practical, almost gentle in its bluntness. “I have a kit where I can tell what certain fluids are. Drugs. Human fluid. Basic chemicals.”
Celty went still.
Not offended. Caught between relief that he was finally in the same register as the photos and the ugly intimacy of being asked whether she had taken her own home apart with a blade or a cloth.
The private channel came first, quieter than the apology.
I photographed. I did not collect. I stood there and I… I just cleaned around it. I didn’t want to touch it more than I had to.
Then she used the PDA, as if the second version might sound less raw.
Celty:
No samples.
Only pictures.
I can go back.
A short pause. The empty line of her neck stayed on him.
“If you can tell from a kit, I will get you what you need.” Black smoke rose in a thin ribbon. “I came here because I trust your eyes more than their excuses. If that means I have to scrape my own furniture, I will. Just—tell me what to take. And tell me what you already think it is, even before the kit. You sat at that table with her. You smelled something I can’t.”
She did not look away.
“I can handle the answer, Curtis. I could not handle going home and pretending I imagined the marks.”
Curtis motioned to her.
“Celty. Come here.”
She crossed the room. He tipped the kitchen chair toward the light, red cap angling as he pointed out a stain that, at a glance, could have belonged to the same ugly family as the ones in her photos.
“This stain came from coffee with cream.” A short, even pause. “Notice how the splash is the same. Normally you couldn’t tell the difference, but under a telescope I am able to.”
He glanced at her, not unkind.
“I might not be on Shinra’s level. Nowhere close. But I can tell what certain things are.”
Celty looked from his chair to the pictures in her hand.
The private channel came through tight, then steadier, as if she were forcing herself not to snatch the worst conclusion just to end the not-knowing.
So the photos are not enough by themselves. You’re saying I could be looking at coffee. Or not. And you will not name it from a picture because the splash can lie.
She stood over his ordinary kitchen stain longer than the object deserved.
“That helps,” she said, quieter. “And it does not help. I wanted you to look at my floor and tell me I was right. Instead you’re telling me I need a sample, because a mark can impersonate another mark.”
A short beat. Black smoke thinned.
“Fine. Then I go back and scrape. Coffee with cream is a kinder answer than the one I brought here. I still need the true one.” Her gloved hand closed around the phone. “If your kit can separate them, I will get you the material. I don’t want Shinra’s level. I want a result he cannot talk me out of.”
She looked at Curtis, at the cleaner evening clothes, at the boy who would not guess just to comfort her.
“Thank you for showing me this first. I would have hated you a little if you had confirmed it from a photo and been wrong. I would have hated the night more if you had called it coffee to be gentle.”
A last, lower line in the link:
Get the kit ready. I’m going to bring you something I wish I didn’t have to touch.
Curtis kept his hand on the back of the kitchen chair, red cap tipping as he made the method and the fear occupy the same sentence.
“Celty, I don’t make conclusions based on assumptions. I need full evidence.” A short, rougher pause. “But I sincerely hope we are both wrong on what we are thinking…”
That was the part that landed.
Not the telescope. Not the coffee-with-cream lookalike. The we. He had not named the stain. He had admitted they were thinking the same thing, and that he wanted that thought to fail the test.
Celty went very still.
Black smoke rose once and held, the private channel opening on something thinner than anger.
So you are thinking it. You just won’t crown it without a sample.
A breath she did not need and took anyway.
“I wanted you to be the person who told me I was imagining it.” Her voice came through low. “Instead you’re the person who hopes with me and still gets the kit ready. That is worse and better at the same time.”
She looked at his face under the cap, at the boy who had smelled Mei at sushi and said nothing, who had refused her address, who was now standing in a clean kitchen hoping a stain was cream.
“I hope we’re wrong too.” The words were quiet, almost unsteady. “If the kit says coffee, I will take the embarrassment. I will take Shinra laughing. I will take Mei calling me territorial over furniture.” A sharper beat. “If it does not say coffee… then I need you not to disappear into manners. I need you to stay in the result with me.”
She turned toward the door, then stopped.
“I’m going to get the sample. Keep hoping, Curtis. Just don’t hope so hard that you stop seeing what is actually on the cloth.”
Curtis’s hand left the chair.
“Celty. I won’t lie to you.”
The line came out without ornament, red cap shadowing eyes that had already refused to call the pictures coffee just to keep her night intact.
Celty’s answer was immediate.
“I know, Curtis. That’s why I came to you.”
The suite held that for a second. Not a fortune. Not a doorway hug. A reason. She had walked past Shinra’s explanations, past Mei’s laugh, past her own inability to smell the house she lived in, and chosen the one person who would not make the stain kinder than the evidence.
Curtis nodded once.
“Then go get the sample. I’ll have the kit ready.” A short pause, quieter. “When it comes back, you get the true version. Even if we both hate it. I’m not going to dress it up as cream if it isn’t, and I’m not going to call it the worst thing if the slide says otherwise.”
He looked at her properly.
“You came to me because I won’t lie. I’ll stay that person after the result too. Not just while we’re still hoping.”
Celty’s private voice came through thinner, almost relieved despite the errand waiting at her own address.
That’s all I needed to hear.
She took the helmet off the table, black smoke settling as she turned for the door.
“I’ll be back. Don’t wash that hope off before I am.”
Then she was gone into the night, leaving Curtis in the furnished quiet with a kit to prepare, a second cup still in the sink, and the promise that whatever she scraped off Shinra’s furniture would not be translated into a kinder lie.
Curtis showed her the door.
The host version of him came back only for that much: red cap tipping as he held it open, the kit already a next task in his head, the second cup still unwashed in the kitchen behind them. Celty paused on the threshold with the helmet under her arm, the private channel quiet now that the promise had been made.
“I’m going up for a quick smoke,” he said.
Not an escape this time. A pause. The roof instead of a lie.
Celty nodded once.
She went downstairs to her bike.
Shooter waited in the dark like it always did — motorcycle to anyone who did not know better, horse to the woman who had crossed an ocean on it. Black smoke kissed the frame as she mounted, the sample errand already turning her toward home, toward a couch and a desk chair and a floor she did not want to touch.
Above, Curtis took the stairs to the roof with a pack in his hand.
Below, Celty’s engine — or what the city mistook for one — cut into the street.
They split on the same night for two different kinds of proof: him with smoke and a kit, her with a scrape she hoped would come back cream, both of them still hoping they were wrong and both of them done pretending hope was the same thing as an answer.
The roof wind took the first drag and did nothing to make the pictures smaller.
Curtis leaned on the ledge, red cap pulled low, long black braids shifting against the black shirt as he stared at Ikebukuro like the city might confess on Shinra’s behalf.
“…Fuckin’ Shinra. I hope that’s coffee.”
The line went into the dark and stayed there. Coffee with cream. Same splash. A kinder answer. The one that would let Celty go home without having to rebuild the rooms she already could not smell.
Justice, in spirit, absolutely did not help.
Dude. This is fucked.
A pause, then the old laugh — the one that had always shown up at the worst possible memorial.
Remember when I got caught cheating on a girl and they both stabbed me? Hahaha. Good times.
Curtis, who could not hear him, took another drag and kept hoping out loud at a doctor who was not on the roof.
Justice kept going anyway, cheerful in the way only the dead could afford.
Yeah, laugh now. You’re the one about to run a fluid kit for a headless woman because her man can’t keep his classmate off the office chair. At least when I got stabbed it was honest. This is worse. This is furniture.
Curtis exhaled smoke toward the street Celty had just taken.
“Please be coffee.”
Unheard, Justice snorted.
Sure, bro. And I’m sure those two girls were just really passionate about cutlery.
Justice was not finished.
Look on the bright side. At least you weren’t doing it with Anri or Rio when she walked in.
Curtis walked away from the ledge.
No answer. No groan. No not now. He killed the cigarette, red cap tipping as he took the stairs back down toward the kit like the joke had not been made. The later hour’s doorway hug, the second cup, Celty in the open door — he was not going to stand on a roof and let a dead brother turn that near-miss into comedy while a sample was still on its way.
Justice called after him anyway.
Aw, c’mon man! I’m kidding. Ah, you party pooper.
A beat, then the grin in the dark, unkillable.
I’m coming.
Curtis unlocked the suite, put the pack down, and started laying out the kit on the kitchen table with the same practical care he had shown the coffee-with-cream chair. Slides. Reagents. The small hard case that did not belong in a furnished love-story apartment and belonged exactly in this one.
Justice occupied the extra stool no one else could see, still talking, still uninvited, still family.
Fine. Be responsible. Run your little science fair. I’ll sit here and not say the thing about the suspenders. …I’m saying it. You confessed in that outfit and now you’re testing couch juice. Iconic night, bro.
Curtis did not answer.
He readied the kit, hoped it was coffee, and let the unheard brother haunt the kitchen, because some company you did not get to send back downstairs to a bike.
